1.1.text:
Article 6
(1) Prevention of violence against women as referred to in Article 5 letter a is carried out in an integrated manner coordinated by the Regional Apparatus responsible for women's empowerment and child protection.
(2) Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by involving:
a. family;
b. community;
c. private sector;
d. community institutions;
e. social institutions; and
f. educational institutions.

Article 8
(1) Protection Services for Women Victims of Violence as referred to in Article 5 letter b shall be provided to women residents of the Region and/or women who experience violence with the location of the incident in the Region.
(2) The implementation of Protection Services for Women Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out with the principles of:
a. swift;
b. safe;
c. a sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easily accessible; and
f. its confidentiality is guaranteed.

Article 9
(1) The Forms of Protection Services for Women Victims of Violence as referred to in Article 8 include:
a. legal aid services;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation services;
d. repatriation and social reintegration services;
e. complaint, consultation, and counseling services; and/or
f. assistance services.
(2) In addition to the Protection Services for Women Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1), Services are carried out by receiving and sending case referrals from and/or to other service units in a networked manner.
(3) The implementation of Protection Services for Women Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by the Regional Apparatus responsible for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.
(4) Further provisions regarding the forms and procedures of Protection Services for Women Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) through paragraph (3) shall be regulated by a Mayor Regulation.

Article 3
Protection of women and children victims of violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. gender responsiveness;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining privacy and confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of safety and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of the victim;
i. being sensitive to the background and conditions of the victim;
j. use of appropriate language that is understood by the victim;
k. fast and simple;
l. empathy; and
m. the best interests of the victim.

Article 5
The stages of protection for women victims of violence include: a. prevention of violence against women;
b. protection services for women victims of violence; and c. empowerment of women victims of violence.

MAYOR OF BANJAR,

Considering:

a. that every woman and child has the right to survival, growth, and proper development as well as the right to protection from violence and discrimination as mandated in the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

b. that acts of violence against women and children in Banjar City have been increasing from year to year, making it the responsibility of the Regional Government of Banjar City to guarantee the protection, fulfillment, and respect for the rights of women and children;

c. that Banjar City does not yet have a regional regulation governing the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence, therefore a comprehensive legal basis is needed in the context of fulfilling the rights of women and children;

d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as has been amended several times, most recently by Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);

3. Law Number 27 of 2002 concerning the Establishment of Banjar City in West Java Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 130, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4246);

4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);

5. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as has been amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

With Joint Approval

CHAPTER VIII
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 25
(1) The community may participate in the protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through:
a. provision of safe houses and/or shelters;
b. establishment and management of orphanages for children;
c. establishment of child rehabilitation centers;
d. active supervision of violence against women and children and reporting to the competent authorities;
e. providing first aid to victims;
f. reporting the occurrence of violence against women and children and providing protection;
g. forming family partners at the village level by the community; and
h. other forms of community participation related to the implementation of protection of women and protection of children.

CHAPTER V
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS
Article 16
(1) Every Victim has the right to:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth as a human being respected;
b. the right to health and psychological recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality of their identity;
f. the right to compensation from the perpetrator;
g. the right to Social Rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right to obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and/or
j. the right to assistance.
(2) In addition to the rights as referred to in paragraph (1), Child Victims of Violence have the right to:
a. the right to respect for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigmas; and/or
e. the right to freedom.

Article 13
Efforts to prevent Violence against Children as referred to in Article 12 shall be carried out by involving: a. parents and/or family;
b. the community;
c. educational institutions;
d. child social welfare institutions; and/or
e. other institutions engaged in the field of concern for child protection.

